Moving the "Accept" and "Reject" buttons inside the View Modal is a massive UX upgrade. It forces the user to actually review the details, the 500-word description, and the history *before* they make a decision.
By placing the buttons inside the <DialogActions> component of Material UI, they will become **"Sticky"**. This means even if the user scrolls through a massive workflow history, the Accept/Reject buttons will stay locked perfectly at the bottom of the screen!
Here are the 3 steps to implement this perfectly.
### Step 1: Remove the buttons from the DataGrid (Grid Cleanup)
Find the actions column in your columns array. Replace it entirely with this simplified version that only contains the Eye icon:
```javascript
    {
      field: "actions",
      headerName: "Action",
      width: 120, // Reduced width since there is only one button now
      align: "center",
      headerAlign: "center",
      disableColumnMenu: true,
      sortable: false,
      renderCell: (params) => {
        return (
          <Tooltip title="View Details & Process Batch">
            <IconButton onClick={() => handleOpenViewModal(params.row)} color="primary">
              <VisibilityIcon />
            </IconButton>
          </Tooltip>
        );
      },
    },

```
### Step 2: Make the View Modal auto-close after success
When a user clicks Accept/Reject in the modal, types their comment, and hits submit, we want *both* modals to close automatically.
Find your handleSubmitAction function. Add setIsViewModalOpen(false); right under the success notification:
```javascript
      showSnackBar(response?.message || "Workflow action processed successfully.", "success");

      handleCloseModal(); // Closes the small comments modal
      setIsScheduleLapseModalOpen(false);
      setIsViewModalOpen(false); // <--- NEW: Closes the Big View Modal
      setNewScheduleTime("");
      fetchPendingBatches();

```
### Step 3: Create the Sticky Footer in the View Modal
Scroll all the way down to your <Dialog open={isViewModalOpen}>.
We are going to move the Download button *outside* of the scrolling <Box> and put it into <DialogActions>, alongside the new Accept and Reject buttons.
**Replace the entire bottom section of your Dialog (starting right after the History Section) with this:**
```javascript
            {/* ... (Your existing Action History Section code ends here) ... */}
            
          </Box>
        </DialogContent>

        {/* --- NEW: STICKY BOTTOM ACTION BAR --- */}
        <DialogActions sx={{ 
          p: 2, 
          backgroundColor: "#ffffff", 
          borderTop: "1px solid", 
          borderColor: "divider", 
          display: "flex", 
          justifyContent: "space-between" 
        }}>
          
          {/* Left Side: Download Button */}
          <Button
            variant="outlined"
            startIcon={<FileDownloadIcon />}
            onClick={handleDownloadExcel}
            disabled={!viewingBatchId}
            sx={{ borderColor: "#217346", color: "#217346", "&:hover": { backgroundColor: "#e8f5e9", borderColor: "#1e6b40" } }}
          >
            Download Report
          </Button>

          {/* Right Side: Accept & Reject Actions */}
          <Stack direction="row" spacing={2} alignItems="center">
            
            {/* Security Notice: Explains WHY buttons are disabled if they are the creator */}
            {isViewingSelfCreated && (viewingBatchState !== "POSTED" && viewingBatchState !== "APPROVED" && viewingBatchState !== "REJECTED") && (
              <Typography variant="caption" color="error.main" fontWeight="bold">
                * Cannot approve own request
              </Typography>
            )}

            <Button
              variant="contained"
              color="error"
              // Disable if it's their own batch OR if the workflow is already finished
              disabled={isViewingSelfCreated || viewingBatchState === "POSTED" || viewingBatchState === "SCHEDULED" || viewingBatchState === "APPROVED" || viewingBatchState === "REJECTED"}
              onClick={() => handleOpenModal(viewingBatch, "REJECTED")}
              sx={{ minWidth: 100 }}
            >
              Reject
            </Button>
            
            <Button
              variant="contained"
              color="success"
              disabled={isViewingSelfCreated || viewingBatchState === "POSTED" || viewingBatchState === "SCHEDULED" || viewingBatchState === "APPROVED" || viewingBatchState === "REJECTED"}
              onClick={() => handleOpenModal(viewingBatch, "ACCEPTED")}
              sx={{ minWidth: 100 }}
            >
              Accept
            </Button>
          </Stack>

        </DialogActions>
      </Dialog>

```
### Why this is the perfect workflow:
 1. **Clean Grid:** The table is now much cleaner without cluttered buttons on every row.
 2. **Review First:** Users are forced to click the Eye icon, letting them scroll through the History and the 500-word description.
 3. **Sticky Controls:** The buttons are locked safely to the bottom of the screen (DialogActions), so they don't move when the user scrolls.
 4. **Trigger Comments:** Clicking "Accept" instantly pops open your existing Remarks dialog, asking them for the compulsory comment before the backend API is triggered!
